# registry-harvest.timer — results registry. Rendered for the site and installed into ~/.config/systemd/user/ by the registry installer.
# Secret-free: the registry needs no credential; ssh (when used) goes through the agent socket.
# Bounded: Type=oneshot with TimeoutStartSec, Restart=no (the timer is the only re-fire; an on-failure restart re-ran a failing
# oneshot every RestartSec and kept it `activating` through every paused window, 2026-09-20), and a start-rate limit (30 starts
# per 5 min: the ceiling is well above the cadence below, so it trips only on a unit that fails in a tight loop and shows in the
# journal and on the status page instead of looping). Locks: only the registry's own (<root>/harvester.lock,
# snapshots/.readers.lock) — never the paper builder's.
#
# Cadence (2026-09-30): OnUnitInactiveSec=30s — the next cycle starts 30 s after the previous one ENDS. The unit ran with
# OnUnitActiveSec=20s, which counts from the previous START: a cycle took 75–110 s on the live registry, so the timer re-fired
# while the cycle was still running and the harvester held <root>/harvester.lock 73 % of a measured hour (17:00–18:00Z); the
# harvester itself lost its own 30-s lock wait 29 times to another holder, and the judging drivers' claims (a 30-s wait) died
# `LockHeld` about 30 times per tick. Counting from deactivation guarantees every cycle is followed by a 30-s window in which
# the lock is free for the other writers. OnBootSec stays: OnUnitInactiveSec never elapses for a service that has not yet
# deactivated in this manager's lifetime, and an OnBootSec already in the past fires at once when the timer is (re)started —
# the landing window's `systemctl --user start registry-harvest.timer` gets its first cycle immediately.
[Unit]
Description=Results registry harvester, 30 s after each cycle ends

[Timer]
OnBootSec=1min
OnUnitInactiveSec=30s
AccuracySec=1s
Unit=registry-harvest.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
